# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/
fuzz/corpus/
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/target/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node artefacts. Never commit these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A running node writes them into its --home; with no --home that is the
# working directory, and during `cargo test` that was once the package root.
# That is how `identity.wrapped`, `kek.params`, `corpus.krab` and a plaintext
# `peer.pad` reached this repository and stayed in its history until the
# purge — see SECURITY-HYGIENE.md.
#
# **This list is not maintained by hand.** It was, and it went stale exactly
# the way `wipe`'s did: written when six artefacts existed, and ten more were
# added over the following months without anyone thinking about this file. The
# test `every_artifact_is_gitignored` in apps/krab-tui/src/artifact.rs asks git
# whether each `Artifact` and `PeerFile` variant is ignored, so a new variant
# fails the suite until it appears below.
#
# The suffix patterns are a second layer for artefacts that do not exist yet.
# A file this program writes should match one of them by name; if it does not,
# the test is what catches it.

# Every Artifact variant, by name.
identity.wrapped
kek.params
corpus.krab
ceremony.cbor
peer.card
peer.pad
prekeys.ring
channels.roster
groups.sealed
reseal.cbor
duress.wrapped
introductions.spent

# Every PeerFile variant. They live in `peers/<node-id>/`, and that whole
# directory is node state — the list of who this node has peered with is a
# disclosure even where the files are sealed.
peers/

# The pre-per-peer-directory layout, still on disk for a node upgraded in
# place. `artifact::wiped` matches these for the same reason.
*.link
*.reservoir

# Suffix patterns, for the artefact nobody has written yet.
*.wrapped
*.card
*.pad
*.ring
*.sealed
*.roster
*.spent
*.krab
kek.*
